SDSS photometry and SEGUE parameters of NGC 2420
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
js/data/observations/sdssNgc2420Photometry.js
js/data/observations/sdssNgc2420Segue.js
Derived from Data Release 18 of the Sloan Digital Sky Survey, queried through
its SkyServer: the stars SDSS's photometry measured cleanly within 14.14
arcminutes of the open cluster NGC 2420, and the SEGUE Stellar Parameter
Pipeline's velocities, temperatures, gravities and metallicities for the stars
SEGUE observed within 30 arcminutes of it.

These are SDSS public data, under the terms and with the acknowledgments given
for the SDSS stellar spectra above: the SDSS-V acknowledgment for Data Release
18, and the SDSS-I/II acknowledgment for the imaging and the SEGUE spectra.
The queries, SHA-256 checksums of the answers SkyServer gave (in a canonical
form that drops nothing but blank lines), and every reduction are recorded in
data-packs/sdss-dr18-ngc2420-photometry.json and
data-packs/sdss-dr18-ngc2420-segue.json. `npm run packs:provenance` rebuilds
the modules from the cached answers and compares byte for byte.

  Almeida et al. (2023), ApJS 267, 44, doi:10.3847/1538-4365/acda98 - DR18
  York et al. (2000), AJ 120, 1579, doi:10.1086/301513 - the survey
  Yanny et al. (2009), AJ 137, 4377, doi:10.1088/0004-6256/137/5/4377 - SEGUE
  Lee et al. (2008a), AJ 136, 2022, doi:10.1088/0004-6256/136/5/2022 - the
  SEGUE Stellar Parameter Pipeline
  Lee et al. (2008b), AJ 136, 2050, doi:10.1088/0004-6256/136/5/2050 - its
  validation on clusters, NGC 2420 among them
  An et al. (2008), ApJS 179, 326, doi:10.1086/592090 - why SDSS's standard
  photometry misses the crowded cores of clusters

